window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"k":"lesson","t":"01 Control Flow","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/01-control-flow.html","x":"Branches (if, switch) decide which statements run; loops (for, while, do-while) repeat them; break and continue steer the loops. Branching (if / else if / else, switch) picks which statements run. Loops (for, range for, while, do-while) repeat them. In a condition, any non-zero number or non-null pointer counts as true; 0 and nullptr count as false. && and || short-circuit: the right side runs only if it can still change the result, so p && p->x is safe when p is null. switch works on integers, char and enums (not std::string, not double); case labels must be constants. A case that declares a variable needs { }. switch needs break in each case or it falls through to the next case. Mark deliberate fall-through with [[fallthrough]];. for (init; condition; step) for counting, range for (x : container) to visit every element, while when the number of passes is unknown. do-while runs the body at least once (condition checked after). break leaves the innermost loop or switch; continue jumps to the next pass. To leave nested loops, use a flag or return from a function. Classic bugs: = instead of ==, a stray ; after if (...) or for (...), <= instead of < (off by one). -Wall -Wextra catches most of them. if, else if, else Logical operators and short-circuit evaluation switch Loops: for, range for, while, do-while break, continue and nested loops Common mistakes while vs do-while = vs == break vs continue && vs & switch vs if-else chain"},{"k":"question","t":"What does this code print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q1","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When should you use a do-while loop instead of a while loop?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q2","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does this compile, and what is the value of x after the if?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q3","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When is switch fall-through intentional rather than a mistake?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q4","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q5","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a while loop always runs its body at least once.","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q6","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q7","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q8","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can be the value tested by a switch? Select all that apply.","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q9","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: inside a for loop, a break placed in a switch leaves the for loop.","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q10","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does this fail to compile, and how do you fix it?","c":"01 Control Flow","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q11","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"02 Pointers","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/02-pointers.html","x":"A pointer is a variable that holds a memory address: & takes an address, * goes to it. A pointer holds an address. &x gives the address of x; *p dereferences p (reads or writes the value at that address). Unlike a reference (lesson 4), a pointer can be reseated and can be null. Declare one pointer per line: in int* a, b; only a is a pointer, b is a plain int. Initialize every pointer, to an address or to nullptr. An uninitialized (wild) pointer holds garbage; dereferencing it or a null pointer is undefined behaviour. Prefer nullptr to NULL or 0. Every data pointer has the same size (8 bytes on x86-64). The type says how to read *p and how far p + 1 moves: sizeof(T) bytes. An array name decays to a pointer to its first element. Pass &x to a function taking int* and the function can change the caller's x (and nullptr can mean \"no value\"). const int* p: the value can't be changed through p. int* const p: p can't be moved. Read the declaration right to left. The pointer variable lives wherever it is declared: on the stack for a local, in static storage for a global, inside its object for a member. What it points to may be anywhere. int** is a pointer to a pointer. void* is an address with no type: cast it back to the real type before use. & and *: taking an address and dereferencing nullptr and wild pointers Pointer size, type and arithmetic Passing a pointer to a function const with pointers Where the pointer itself lives Pointer to pointer and void* Common mistakes * vs & p vs &p null vs dangling vs wild const int* vs int* const nullptr vs NULL / 0"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between *p and &x?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q12","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"For a heap pointer p, what does printing p give you versus printing &p?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q13","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a null pointer and a dangling pointer?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q14","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Can a pointer be changed to point somewhere else after it is created?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q15","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Where does a pointer variable itself live in memory?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q16","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"After int x = 5; int* p = &x; *p = 9; what is the value of x?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q17","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q18","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q19","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In int* a, b;, what is the type of b?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q20","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given int x = 1, y = 2; int* const p = &x;, which lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q21","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on x86-64 with g++, sizeof(char*) is smaller than sizeof(double*).","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q22","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why prefer nullptr over NULL or 0 for a null pointer?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q23","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function int count(int arr[]) returns sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]). Called with a 10-element array on x86-64, it returns 2. Why, and what is the fix?","c":"02 Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q24","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/03-stack-and-heap.html","x":"A process's memory has regions: code, globals, heap and stack. The stack is fast, small and automatic; the heap is big, flexible and must be freed. A running program's memory is split into regions: text (machine code), data / bss (globals and statics), heap (new / malloc), stack (local variables and call frames). Stack: each function call pushes a frame (return address, saved registers, locals); returning pops it. Allocating is just moving the stack pointer: fast, automatic, lifetime tied to the scope. The stack's address range is set aside in advance, but each page gets physical RAM only when first touched. It is small: 8 MB for the main thread on typical Linux (ulimit -s = 8192 KiB). Too deep recursion or a huge local array = stack overflow (segfault). On x86-64 the stack is at high addresses (0x7ff...) and grows DOWN: a push decrements the stack pointer, so the 'top of stack' is the lowest address. Heap: any size, chosen at run time, lives until freed. The allocator must find a free block and keep bookkeeping, so it is slower, and the heap can fragment. Use the stack by default (small, fixed size, needed only in this scope); use the heap for big data, sizes known only at run time, or data that must outlive the function. Heap memory nobody frees is a leak. Modern C++ lets an owner object free it: std::vector, std::string, smart pointers (RAII). Returning &local = dangling pointer (UB); mismatched new / delete[] = UB. ASLR moves every region to a random address on each run. The memory layout of a process The stack: frames, and why it is fast How big the stack is, and stack overflow The heap: flexible but slower Stack vs heap: which one to use Memory leaks, and the modern answer Common mistakes stack vs heap grows down vs 'top' &ptr vs ptr data vs bss"},{"k":"question","t":"Why is stack allocation faster than heap allocation?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q25","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which direction does the stack grow, and where is its 'top'?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q26","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens when the caller dereferences the pointer this function returns?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q27","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens if you free new[] memory with plain delete (instead of delete[])?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q28","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does very deep recursion crash a program?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q29","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do the heap addresses a program prints change on every run?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q30","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In void f() { int a = 1; int* p = new int(2); }, what lives on the heap?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q31","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the stack grows toward lower addresses, so the most recently pushed value sits at the lowest address.","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q32","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A global int total; with no initializer: which region holds it?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q33","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on Linux, the whole 8 MB stack is given physical RAM when the program starts.","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q34","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does a stack frame hold, and what happens to it when the function returns?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q35","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these belong on the heap rather than the stack? Select all that apply.","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q36","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you see the stack size limit on Linux, and how does a program end when it overflows the stack?","c":"03 Stack & Heap Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q37","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"04 References","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/04-references.html","x":"A reference is another name for an existing object, bound once at declaration: pass big objects by const reference, and never return one to a local. A reference is an alias (another name) for an existing object: after int& r = x;, using r is using x, and &r == &x. It must be initialized when declared and is fixed from then on: r = y; copies y's value into x, it does not rebind r. It cannot be null (in well-formed code) and has no arithmetic. Pass-by-value = copy (changes don't escape); pass-by-reference (int&) = writes go straight to the caller's variable. const T& = read-only alias with no copy: the usual way to pass big objects (std::string, std::vector). It can also bind to a temporary (const int& r = 5;); a plain int& can't. Range-for: auto x copies each element, auto& x lets you modify them, const auto& x reads without copying. Return a reference only to something that outlives the call: a member, a static, an element of a container the caller owns. A reference to a local dangles (UB). Under the hood a reference is usually an address, but sizeof(r) and &r give the size and address of the object it refers to. Use a reference when there must be an object; use a pointer when \"no object\" (nullptr) or re-pointing is needed. A reference is another name Passing by reference const references References in range-for loops Returning a reference How references work underneath Pointer vs reference Common mistakes reference vs pointer pass-by-value vs pass-by-reference T& vs const T& auto vs auto& vs const auto& in range-for r = y vs rebinding"},{"k":"question","t":"Name two things a reference cannot do that a pointer can.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q38","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print, and why?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q39","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do we often pass large objects as const T& instead of by value?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q40","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must a reference be initialized at the point where it is declared?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q41","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q42","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: in well-formed code, a reference can be null.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q43","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q44","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q45","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which line does not compile? (x is an int variable.)","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q46","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: with std::array<int, 100> a; auto& r = a;, sizeof(r) is 8 on x86-64, the size of a pointer.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q47","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are good reasons to use a pointer instead of a reference? Select all that apply.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q48","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Returning int& from a member function like int& at(int i) { return values[i]; } is fine, but returning int& to a local variable is a bug. Explain the difference.","c":"04 References","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q49","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/05-classes-encapsulation.html","x":"A class bundles data with the functions that work on it. Encapsulation keeps the data private, so every change goes through a member function that keeps the object valid. A class is a user-defined type: data members + member functions. An object is one instance of it; each object has its own copy of the data, and the functions are shared code. Access specifiers: public = anyone; private = only the class's own member functions; protected = the class and its derived classes (lesson 13). Access is checked per class, not per object. An invariant is a rule that must always hold for a valid object (balance >= 0, minute in 0..59). Private data + public methods = every change forced through a single gate that validates it, so the object can never reach an invalid state. The constructor makes the object valid from the first moment: it checks its arguments and refuses (throws) if they would break the invariant. Getters and setters that only read and write a field are noise. A setter helps when it validates; better still are real operations (deposit, withdraw, addMinutes). private isn't about security against attackers: it is controlled change, plus freedom to change the internals later without breaking users. Cost: more code and some interface design. For a plain bundle of values with no rule between them, public data is fine (a struct, lesson 6). What a class is Access specifiers: public, private, protected Invariants, and the constructor that establishes them Getters, setters and real operations Advantages and costs of encapsulation Common mistakes private vs security encapsulation vs abstraction class vs object private vs protected validating setter vs real operation"},{"k":"question","t":"If private gives no runtime security, what is the real point of making data private?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q50","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a class invariant?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q51","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Show how a method can protect an invariant that a public field could not.","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q52","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q53","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: making a member private stops other code from reading that object's memory at runtime.","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q54","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q55","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Inside a member function of class Player, can you read the private member health of another Player object passed as a parameter?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q56","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: adding a member function to a class makes every object of that class bigger.","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q57","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is a class with a public getter and an unchecked setter for every field hardly better than a struct with public fields? What should you write instead?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q58","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What role does the constructor play in keeping a class invariant?","c":"05 Classes & Encapsulation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q59","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"06 Classes vs Structs","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/06-classes-vs-structs.html","x":"struct and class differ only in their defaults (public vs private); everything else is identical, and the choice between them is a convention. Only two real language differences: (1) default member access: struct public, class private; (2) default inheritance: struct public, class private. Everything else is identical: both can have constructors, methods, private members, virtual functions, inheritance and templates. A struct may have private: members and a class public: ones. template<class T> / template<typename T> are allowed; template<struct T> is not. A plain struct (public data, no constructors, no virtual functions) is an aggregate: Point p{1, 2}; sets the members in order, and members left out become 0. A C struct holds only data, and its type is written struct Point (unless typedef'd). A C++ struct is a full class, and Point alone names it. Convention: struct = passive bundle of public data with no rule between the members; class = encapsulated type with behaviour and invariants. The two real differences Everything else is the same Aggregate initialization C struct vs C++ struct When to use which Common mistakes class default vs struct default aggregate vs class with a constructor Point p; vs Point p{}; C struct vs C++ struct"},{"k":"question","t":"What are the ONLY two language-level differences between a class and a struct?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q60","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is x accessible from outside here?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q61","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"By convention, when do you use a struct rather than a class?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q62","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are real language differences between class and struct? Select all that apply.","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q63","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"struct B { int x; }; and class D : B {};. Does D d; d.x = 1; compile?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q64","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q65","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these types can be initialized with T t{1, 2}; as an aggregate (member by member)?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q66","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a C compiler accepts struct Point { int x; void move(); };.","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q67","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"struct Point { int x, y; }; was used as Point p{1, 2}; all over a project. Someone adds Point(int v) : x(v), y(v) {}. Why do those lines stop compiling, and what are two fixes?","c":"06 Classes vs Structs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q68","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"07 How to Write a Class","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/07-writing-a-class.html","x":"Design a class in steps (private data, invariant, constructor, operations, const getters), then split it into a guarded header and a source file. Decide what's private (data) and what's public (interface); outsiders touch data only through member functions. Design order: private data, then the invariant it must keep, a constructor that establishes it, public operations that keep it, and const getters. A member function defined inside the class body is implicitly inline. Defined outside, it needs the class name: long BankAccount::balance() const { ... }. Forgetting ClassName:: defines an unrelated free function: a compile error if its body uses members ('x' was not declared in this scope), otherwise the member stays undefined and the link fails (undefined reference). Header/source split: the class definition goes in a .h, member function bodies in one .cpp. A non-inline body in a header included by several .cpp files breaks the One Definition Rule: multiple definition at link time. Every header needs an include guard (#ifndef X_H / #define X_H / #endif) or #pragma once. Without one, a header included twice in one .cpp gives redefinition of 'class X'. const-correctness: a member function that doesn't modify the object should be const (long balance() const). Only const functions can be called on const objects and const& parameters. Conventions: public section first, private data last; mark data members (m_balance or balance_); and end the class with };. Designing a class step by step const member functions Splitting the class into a header and a source file Include guards, #pragma once and the One Definition Rule Conventions Common mistakes declaration vs definition inline member vs Class:: include guard vs ODR #ifndef guard vs #pragma once const member function vs const return"},{"k":"question","t":"Why does this compile but fail to link with 'undefined reference to Entity::GetX()'?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q69","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When should a member function be marked const?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q70","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens if you put a non-inline function's full body in a header that multiple .cpp files include?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q71","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a declaration and a definition?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q72","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Entity declares int GetX() const;. Which out-of-class definition is correct?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q73","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a member function defined inside the class body is implicitly inline.","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q74","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q75","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"point.h has no include guard. main.cpp includes point.h and also shape.h, which itself includes point.h. What happens when you build?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q76","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A member is declared in the class as virtual void draw(int scale = 1) const;. Which parts must not be repeated in the out-of-class definition? Select all that apply.","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q77","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: g++ main.cpp -o app is enough to build a program whose member functions are defined in bank_account.cpp.","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q78","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do tiny getters often stay in the class body in the header, while bigger member functions go in the .cpp?","c":"07 How to Write a Class","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q79","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"08 Static (outside classes)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/08-static-outside-classes.html","x":"Outside a class, static makes a global variable or function private to its own .cpp file (internal linkage), and makes a local variable live for the whole program. static at file scope gives internal linkage: the name is visible only in its translation unit (one .cpp plus its headers). Without static, a global variable or function has external linkage: any file can link to it. static works the same on functions: a static helper is private to its file, so two files can each have their own logCall(). Same non-static global defined in two files: multiple definition link error. extern int x; with no definition anywhere: undefined reference. extern only declares; it creates nothing. The unnamed namespace (namespace { ... }) is the modern way to make names file-private. It also works for types, which static can't. One global shared through a header: C++17 inline int x = 1;, or extern int x; in the header plus int x = 1; in one .cpp. A plain int x = 1; in a header breaks the link; static int x = 1; silently gives each file its own copy. A const global already has internal linkage in C++ (not in C). Share it with extern const int x = 10; or inline constexpr in a header. A static local variable is initialized once, keeps its value between calls and lives for the whole program; its scope stays the function (lesson 44). Globals in different files are initialized in an unspecified order (the static initialization order fiasco). Constants or a function-local static avoid it. Translation units and linkage The two classic linker errors The unnamed namespace: the modern alternative Sharing one global through a header (C++17 inline variables) Static local variables Initialization order across files Summary: every kind of global Common mistakes static (file scope) vs extern static local vs normal local declaration vs definition static global vs inline variable in a header static vs unnamed namespace"},{"k":"question","t":"What does calling counter() three times in a row print?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q80","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does static do to a variable declared at file/global scope?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q81","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static local variable initialize safely regardless of other globals' init order?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q82","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does extern int x; declare?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q83","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q84","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"a.cpp has static int helper = 5; at file scope. b.cpp says extern int helper; and uses it. What happens?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q85","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A header contains static int counter = 0; and is included by a.cpp and b.cpp. A function in a.cpp sets counter = 5. What value does code in b.cpp read?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q86","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these, written at file scope in a .cpp, have internal linkage? Select all that apply.","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q87","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: in C++, if limits.cpp defines const int maxUsers = 10; at file scope, another file can use it after declaring extern const int maxUsers;.","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q88","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You want one global setting shared by every .cpp that includes config.h. Which line in the header does that on its own, with no extra definition in a .cpp?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q89","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do modern C++ guidelines prefer an unnamed namespace to static for file-private names?","c":"08 Static (outside classes)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q90","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/09-static-in-classes.html","x":"A static member belongs to the class, not to any object: one shared copy of the data, and functions that run without an object (no this). A static data member has one copy shared by all objects of the class. It lives in static storage, outside the objects, so it does not add to sizeof. Classic use: an instance counter. Declared in the class, it still needs one definition outside it (int Player::alive = 0; in one .cpp), or the link fails with undefined reference. C++17 inline static defines it inside the class. In-class constants: static constexpr int max = 100; (implicitly inline since C++17, usable at compile time). A static member function has no this. Call it as Class::fn() without an object. It can use static members directly, but non-static members only through an object it is given. Use static functions for factory functions (named constructors), utilities that belong to the class, accessors for static data, and callbacks for C APIs that take a plain function pointer. Static data is shared by all objects and all threads. Changing it from two threads at once is a data race: use std::atomic or a mutex. Singleton: a static function returning a static local (static Logger& instance()). Built once on first use, but it is a hidden global: hard to test, so use it sparingly. Static data members: one copy for the whole class Defining static data members Static member functions Shared by every thread too The singleton pattern Common mistakes static member vs instance member static method vs normal method declaration vs definition of a static member static constexpr vs inline static static member vs global variable"},{"k":"question","t":"Does a static data member increase each object's sizeof?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q91","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does this fail to link with 'undefined reference to Entity::count'?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q92","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can't a static member function read a normal (non-static) data member?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q93","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On x86-64, what does this print?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q94","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a static member function can read the object's non-static data members.","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q95","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q96","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these define a static data member inside the class, so no separate definition in a .cpp is needed? Select all that apply.","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q97","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the address of a static member function can be stored in a plain function pointer such as void (*fp)(int).","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q98","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why are factory functions such as Temperature::fromCelsius(double) and Temperature::fromFahrenheit(double) written as static member functions instead of constructors?","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q99","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two drawbacks of the singleton pattern (static Logger& instance()).","c":"09 Static (for Classes & Structs)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q100","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"10 Enums","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/10-enums.html","x":"An enum gives names to a fixed set of integer values. Prefer `enum class`: its names are scoped and it never converts to `int` by accident. An enum is a type whose values are a fixed list of named integer constants. Values start at 0 and count up by 1 unless assigned: enum E { A = 3, B, C }; gives B = 4, C = 5. Stored as an integer, the underlying type: int for an enum class, compiler-chosen for a plain enum (usually 4 bytes with g++). : std::uint8_t makes it 1 byte. Plain enum: names leak into the enclosing scope (two enums can't both have Red) and it converts to int silently. enum class (C++11): names are scoped (Color::Red), no implicit conversion to int or between enums. The default choice. Convert on purpose with static_cast, both ways: static_cast<int>(c), static_cast<Color>(n). Check an incoming int first: a value with no enumerator has no name. C++ can't print an enum's name: write a function with a switch. Without default, -Wall warns when an enumerator isn't handled. Typical uses: states (state machines), options (instead of a bool parameter), bit flags (1, 2, 4 combined with |). What an enum is Plain enum vs enum class Underlying type and sizeof A complete example: enum class with switch Options and bit flags Common mistakes enum vs enum class enum vs enumerator enum vs const int constants switch with vs without default"},{"k":"question","t":"In enum E { A = 3, B, C };, what are the values of B and C?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q101","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two problems with a plain enum that enum class fixes.","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q102","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you make an enum occupy only 1 byte instead of the default 4?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q103","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you convert an enum class value to an int?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q104","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q105","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"enum class Color { Red, Green }; What does int x = Color::Green; do?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q106","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q107","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given enum class Color { Red, Green, Blue }; and enum class Light { Red, Green };, which lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q108","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: with enum class Color { Red, Green, Blue };, the expression static_cast<Color>(7) is undefined behaviour.","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q109","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On x86-64 with g++, what is sizeof(Pixel) for enum class Color : std::uint8_t { Red, Green, Blue }; struct Pixel { Color c; std::uint8_t alpha; };?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q110","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"C++ has no built-in way to print an enum's name. How do you write a name function for an enum class, and why leave out default?","c":"10 Enums","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q111","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"11 Constructors","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/11-constructors.html","x":"A constructor is the special function that runs when an object is created and puts it in a valid state; a class can have several, and the arguments choose which one runs. A constructor has the same name as the class and no return type. It runs automatically when an object is created. Its job: give every member a valid value, acquire resources, and check the rules (invariants). A class can have several constructors (overloading); the arguments pick one: default (no arguments), parameterized, copy T(const T&) (lesson 41) and move T(T&&) (takes over the resources of an object that is about to die). The compiler writes a default constructor only if you declare no constructor at all. Declare any constructor and it is gone; Entity() = default; brings it back. = delete forbids a constructor, for example copying. Default values: default member initializers (int hp = 100;) apply in every constructor that doesn't set the member; default arguments (Rect(int w = 1, int h = 1)) let one constructor be called several ways. The member initializer list (: x(px)) initializes members before the body runs. It is required for const members, references, and members with no default constructor (lesson 32). Brace initialization Entity e{1, 2}; refuses narrowing conversions such as double to int, which parentheses allow silently. Entity e{}; also avoids the most vexing parse: Entity e(); declares a function. A constructor can't return an error: it reports failure by throwing. The object then never exists, and its destructor does not run. Constructors can't be virtual. What a constructor is, and which one runs The default constructor, = default and = delete Default values: default arguments and default member initializers The member initializer list (in short) Brace initialization and narrowing When construction fails Common mistakes Entity e; vs Entity e(); init list vs assignment in body = default vs = delete default constructor vs default arguments copy vs move constructor"},{"k":"question","t":"Why does Entity e; stop compiling once you add a constructor that takes arguments?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q112","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name two cases where a member initializer list is required, not merely preferred.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q113","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why prefer an initializer list over assigning members inside the constructor body?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q114","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does Entity e(); actually declare?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q115","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which line creates a default-constructed Entity object named e?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q116","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which members must be initialized in the member initializer list? Select all that apply.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q117","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q118","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"struct P { int x, y; P(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {} }; What does P p{2.9, 1}; do with g++?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q119","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a constructor throws an exception, the destructor of that object runs to clean up.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q120","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true about constructors? Select all that apply.","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q121","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does FileHandle(const FileHandle&) = delete; do, and why would a class want it?","c":"11 Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q122","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"12 Destructors & RAII","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/12-destructors.html","x":"A destructor runs automatically when an object's lifetime ends; RAII uses that to release every resource on every path, even when an exception is thrown. A destructor is ~ClassName(): no parameters, no return type, exactly one per class. It runs automatically when the object's lifetime ends; you never call it yourself. When it runs: a local at the end of its scope; a heap object at delete (delete[]: every element); a temporary at the end of its full expression (lesson 39); statics after main returns; locals during stack unwinding (exceptions). Order: the reverse of construction. The destructor body runs first, then the members in reverse declaration order, then the base class (derived before base). RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization): acquire a resource in the constructor, release it in the destructor. It is then released on every path: normal end, early return, exception. std::lock_guard, std::unique_ptr, std::vector and std::fstream all work this way. Rule of Zero: build classes from members that clean up themselves (std::string, std::vector, std::unique_ptr) and write no destructor at all. A class that must free a raw resource itself also needs copy and move handling (Rule of Three/Five, lesson 41). A base class used through base pointers needs a virtual destructor: delete through a Base* with a non-virtual destructor is undefined behaviour (usually only the base part is destroyed). Destructors must not throw: they are implicitly noexcept, so an escaping exception calls std::terminate. Never call a destructor explicitly: it would run twice. What a destructor is When destructors run RAII: tie every resource to an object The Rule of Zero Virtual destructors Destructors must not throw; never call one yourself Common mistakes virtual vs non-virtual destructor RAII vs manual cleanup Rule of Zero vs Rule of Three/Five destructor order vs constructor order"},{"k":"question","t":"Base has a non-virtual destructor — what happens when delete b; runs?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q123","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does RAII make resource cleanup automatic and exception-safe?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q124","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the Rule of Three?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q125","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are members and base/derived subobjects destroyed?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q126","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q127","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: deleting a derived object through a base pointer is safe even when the base destructor is not virtual.","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q128","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q129","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function locks a mutex with a std::lock_guard and then throws an exception. When is the mutex unlocked?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q130","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if an exception escapes from a destructor, the nearest matching catch block handles it like any other exception.","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q131","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the Rule of Zero, and why is it preferred?","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q132","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these end an object's lifetime and run its destructor? Select all that apply.","c":"12 Destructors & RAII","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q133","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"13 Inheritance","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/13-inheritance.html","x":"A derived class reuses and extends a base class. Use it for a true is-a relationship; use composition (has-a) for everything else. Inheritance: class Car : public Vehicle gives Car all of Vehicle's members, and Car adds its own. Use it only when the derived class truly is a kind of the base. A derived object contains a base part. Construction runs base first, then derived; destruction runs in reverse. Pass arguments to the base constructor in the initializer list: Car(...) : Vehicle(brand). Not inherited: constructors, destructor, assignment operators. using Vehicle::Vehicle; opts in to the base constructors. public inheritance keeps the base's public members public: the normal is-a. protected/private inheritance hide them (rare: reuse of implementation). protected member = usable by the class and its derived classes, not by outside code. has-a = composition: keep the other object as a member (Car has an Engine). Prefer it when you only want to reuse code or the relationship is \"contains / uses\". Substitution test (Liskov): anything that works with a base object must still work with a derived one. If not (Square/Rectangle), don't inherit. A derived object can be passed where a base is expected (upcast, via pointer or reference). Copying it into a base by value slices it, and a derived function hides every base overload with the same name. What inheritance is Constructors and destruction order Access: public, protected and private inheritance is-a vs has-a: inheritance vs composition The substitution test (Liskov) Upcasting, slicing and name hiding Multiple inheritance and the diamond Common mistakes is-a vs has-a public vs private inheritance protected vs private member hiding vs overriding upcast vs downcast slicing vs polymorphism"},{"k":"question","t":"What is object slicing?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q134","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Who can access a protected member?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q135","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are the base and derived parts constructed and destroyed?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q136","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an 'is-a' and a 'has-a' relationship?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q137","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q138","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q139","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two reasons to choose composition (has-a) over inheritance (is-a) when you only want to reuse another class's code.","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q140","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q141","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Square inherits from Rectangle. A function that sets a rectangle's width to 10 and height to 5 leaves a Square with sides 10 and 5. Which principle does this design break?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q142","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a derived class automatically gets the base class's constructors.","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q143","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are better modelled with composition (has-a) than with inheritance? Select all that apply.","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q144","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With class D : protected B, what does a public member of B become inside D?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q145","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A base class has print() and print(int). The derived class declares only print(). Why does d.print(3) fail to compile, and how do you fix it?","c":"13 Inheritance","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q146","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"14 Virtual Functions","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/14-virtual-functions.html","x":"A virtual function is chosen at run time from the real type of the object, so one piece of code can work with many derived types. Without virtual, basePtr->fn() uses static dispatch: the compiler picks the function from the pointer's type (the base version). With virtual it uses dynamic dispatch: the function of the real object is chosen at run time. Dynamic dispatch works only through a pointer or reference to the base. A by-value copy is sliced (lesson 13) and runs the base version. An override is virtual automatically. Write override on it: a signature mismatch (a missing const) becomes a compile error instead of a silent new function. final on a function stops further overriding; on a class it stops further inheritance. vtable = one table per class of function pointers, one slot per virtual function. vptr = a hidden pointer in each object to its class's vtable. A class with no virtual functions has neither. Cost: a vptr per object (8 bytes on x86-64), an extra load per call, usually no inlining. Traps: a virtual call inside a constructor or destructor does not reach the derived override, and default arguments come from the static type. A class meant to be used through base pointers needs a virtual destructor (lesson 12). Static vs dynamic dispatch Polymorphism: one loop, many types How it works: vtable and vptr override and final Virtual calls in constructors and destructors Default arguments use the static type When to use virtual functions Common mistakes static vs dynamic dispatch vtable vs vptr override vs overload override vs hiding override vs final"},{"k":"question","t":"What does a->speak() print here, and why?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q147","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does a virtual call select the right function at runtime, using the vtable and vptr?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q148","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the cost of using virtual functions?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q149","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the override specifier do, and why is it worth adding?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q150","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does final do in C++?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q151","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q152","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A class has no virtual functions. How much does a vptr add to each object?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q153","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q154","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q155","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Dog derives from Animal, which has virtual void speak() const, and Dog d; exists. Which of these calls print Dog's version? Select all that apply.","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q156","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a function in a derived class that matches a base virtual function's signature is virtual too, even without the virtual keyword.","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q157","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two situations where you would not make a function virtual.","c":"14 Virtual Functions","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q158","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/15-interfaces-abstract-classes.html","x":"A pure virtual function makes a class abstract: it can't be created, only derived from. A class of only pure virtual functions is an interface: a contract other code depends on. Pure virtual function: virtual double area() const = 0; has no default; every concrete derived class must override it. A class with at least one pure virtual function is abstract: you can't create an object of it (Shape s; doesn't compile), not even as a by-value parameter. A derived class becomes concrete only when it overrides every pure virtual function. Until then it is abstract too. You use an abstract class through pointers and references (const Shape&, Shape*): that is the whole point. An abstract class can still have data, constructors and implemented functions: shared code written once, with the type-specific steps left pure. Interface = only pure virtual functions and no data (a convention: C++ has no interface keyword). A class can implement several interfaces. Every polymorphic base, interfaces included, needs a virtual destructor: virtual ~Shape() = default; (lesson 12). Why interfaces: code depends on the contract, not the implementation, so you can swap implementations and test with fakes. Cost: a virtual call and an extra layer. Pure virtual functions and abstract classes Abstract classes can have data, constructors and code Interfaces, and implementing several Why program to an interface: decoupling and testing Concrete base vs abstract class vs interface Common mistakes abstract class vs interface pure virtual vs virtual abstract vs concrete class interface vs implementation"},{"k":"question","t":"What makes a class abstract in C++?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q159","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If you can't instantiate an abstract class, what CAN you do with it?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q160","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface in C++?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q161","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must an interface / polymorphic base declare a virtual destructor?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q162","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Can an abstract class also contain normal (non-pure) methods with bodies?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q163","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; };, which line does not compile?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q164","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a derived class that overrides only some of its base's pure virtual functions is still abstract.","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q165","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q166","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can an abstract class have? Select all that apply.","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q167","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if Shape is abstract, void draw(Shape s) { } compiles.","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q168","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do interfaces make a class easier to unit-test?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q169","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A class declares virtual ~Base() = 0; and never defines it. A class derives from it and an object of the derived class is created. What happens?","c":"15 Interfaces / Abstract Classes","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q170","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/16-const-constexpr-volatile.html","x":"`const` means read-only, `constexpr` means known at compile time, `volatile` means every access must really happen. const = read-only after initialization; the value may come from run time (const int n = argc;). It must be initialized. Pointers: read right to left. const int* p = pointer to a const int (can't change *p, can move p). int* const p = const pointer (can change *p, can't move p). const int* const p = both fixed. const T& parameters: no copy, no modification, and they accept temporaries and literals (a plain T& does not). const member function int get() const: promises not to change the object. Only const member functions can be called on a const object. A mutable member may change even inside them (caches, counters). constexpr variable = value computed at compile time, so it can be used where C++ needs a constant (array sizes, template arguments, static_assert). Every constexpr variable is const, not the other way round. constexpr function: runs at compile time when its arguments are constants and the result is needed there; otherwise at run time like a normal function. consteval (C++20) must run at compile time. volatile = every read and write must really happen; the compiler may not cache or remove them. Real uses: memory-mapped hardware registers and a volatile std::sig_atomic_t flag set by a signal handler. volatile gives no atomicity and no thread ordering. For data shared between threads use std::atomic or a mutex. const variables const with pointers const reference parameters const member functions and mutable constexpr variables and functions volatile: accesses that must really happen const vs constexpr vs volatile Common mistakes const vs constexpr const int* p vs int* const p constexpr function vs consteval function const member function vs mutable member volatile vs std::atomic constexpr vs volatile"},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these compile, and which is an error?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q171","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the core difference between const and constexpr?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q172","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is volatile a correct tool for synchronizing a variable between threads?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q173","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When does a constexpr function run at runtime instead of compile time?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q174","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"n is read at runtime (int n = getInput();). Which of these lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q175","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: marking a shared counter volatile makes ++counter safe to call from two threads.","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q176","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which declaration lets you change the int through p, but never make p point to another int?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q177","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q178","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a consteval function (C++20) can be called with a value read at run time; it then simply runs at run time.","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q179","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A signal handler sets a global flag and main loops while (!stop) {}. Why must the flag be declared volatile std::sig_atomic_t, and what can happen at -O2 without volatile?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q180","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does const after a member function's parameter list (int get() const) promise, and which member functions can be called on a const object?","c":"16 const, constexpr & volatile","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q181","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/17-compile-time-constants-and-vla.html","x":"Why `int arr[n]` with a run-time `n` is not standard C++, and what to use instead. A constant expression is a value the compiler can work out before the program runs. C++ requires one for array sizes, template arguments (std::array<int, N>), case labels, static_assert and enumerator values. constexpr int s = 5*4; int a[s]; and const int s = 5*4; int a[s]; are both standard: a const integer initialized with a constant expression is a constant. const int s = readSize(); is not, so int a[s]; is then a VLA. int s = 5*4; int a[s]; is a VLA (variable length array): its size is decided at run time. Not standard C++ in any version. VLAs are standard in C99 (optional since C11). g++ and clang accept them in C++ as an extension; MSVC does not support them. -pedantic (or -Wvla) makes g++ warn in every -std= mode; -pedantic-errors makes it an error. The flag matters, not the version. VLAs are risky: they live on the stack with no size check, so a large or user-controlled n crashes the program. sizeof of a VLA is computed at run time. Standard replacements: std::vector<int> v(n) (run-time size, heap, can grow), std::make_unique<int[]>(n) (run-time size, fixed), std::array<int, N> (compile-time size). Where C++ requires a constant expression const vs constexpr as an array size VLAs: what g++ accepts and the standard doesn't Standard alternatives Common mistakes const int size vs constexpr int size const int size vs plain int size compiler allows vs standard allows VLA vs std::vector"},{"k":"question","t":"Two of these are standard C++ and one is not — which one, and what is it called?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q182","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Your int a[n] (runtime n) compiled fine on g++ — how do you check whether it's actually standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q183","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does the VLA warning depend on which C++ version you pick (-std=c++11 vs c++20)?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q184","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which array sizes ARE valid standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q185","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What runtime clue reveals that an array is actually a VLA?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q186","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"int a[n]; with a runtime n compiles silently on g++. Which flag makes g++ warn that it is not standard C++?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q187","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: switching from -std=c++11 to -std=c++20 makes g++ reject int a[n]; with a runtime n.","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q188","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: const int n = readSize(); int a[n]; is standard C++, because n is const.","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q189","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In which of these places must the value be a compile-time constant expression? Select all that apply.","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q190","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q191","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program does int buf[n]; where n comes from user input. Explain why this can crash with no error message, and what to write instead.","c":"17 Compile-time Constants & VLAs","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q192","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/18-arrays-and-contiguity.html","x":"A raw array is a fixed number of elements side by side in memory: indexing is one multiply-and-add, iteration is cache-friendly, and the array forgets its size when passed to a function. int a[5] = {10, 20}; makes 5 ints; missing initializers become 0. int b[] = {1, 2, 3}; deduces the size. Indexes run 0 to N-1. std::size(a) gives the element count. A local array with no initializer holds garbage; reading it is undefined behaviour. int c[5] = {}; zeroes it. An array is contiguous: a[i] is *(a + i), the address base + i * sizeof(element). One multiply-and-add, so indexing is O(1). Decay: passed to a function, an array becomes a pointer to its first element and loses its size (sizeof gives 8 on x86-64). Pass the size too, or use a template reference, std::array or std::vector. Raw arrays can't be assigned (a = b doesn't compile), and a == b compares addresses, not elements. A 2D array int m[R][C] is row-major: row 0, then row 1, in one block. Loop row by row (inner loop over the last index). Contiguous data is cache-friendly: the CPU loads memory in 64-byte cache lines and prefetches the next ones, so walking an array in order is much faster than jumping around. Contiguity is needed in virtual address space only; the pages can sit in scattered physical frames. Big arrays belong on the heap (std::vector): the stack is often 8 MB. Raw array basics Contiguity and indexing Array decay: passing arrays to functions 2D arrays are row-major Why contiguity is fast: the cache Where arrays live; virtual vs physical contiguity Common mistakes array vs pointer a[i] vs *(a + i) row-major vs column-major order virtual-contiguous vs physical-scattered stack array vs heap array"},{"k":"question","t":"An array must be laid out contiguously — is that guaranteed in virtual memory or in physical RAM?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q193","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does the compiler compute the address of arr[i]?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q194","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"An int arr[100000] is about 400 KB — should it live on the stack or the heap, and why?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q195","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With demand paging, is the physical RAM for a big new array allocated immediately?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q196","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q197","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"An array must be contiguous. Where is that actually guaranteed?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q198","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q199","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In int m[3][4];, which element is stored directly after m[0][3] in memory?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q200","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: inside void f(int a[10]), sizeof(a) is the size of 10 ints.","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q201","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Summing a 4096 x 4096 int array row by row is many times faster than column by column, although both add the same numbers. Why?","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q202","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two ways to pass an array to a function without losing its size.","c":"18 Arrays & Memory Contiguity","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q203","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"19 Endianness","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/19-endianness.html","x":"The order in which the bytes of a multi-byte value sit in memory. It is invisible in values and visible in raw bytes, files and network data. Endianness = the order of the bytes of a multi-byte value in memory. Little-endian puts the least-significant byte at the lowest address (x86-64, most ARM systems). Big-endian puts the most-significant byte first. We write numbers most-significant first, like big-endian. On little-endian, 0x01020304 is stored 04 03 02 01: reverse the bytes to read the value. Applies to every multi-byte value: short, int, long, float, pointers (a pointer is an 8-byte integer on x86-64). Single bytes (char, uint8_t) are unaffected. Visible only in raw bytes (a unsigned char* walk, a hex dump, a file, network data). cout/printf print the value, never reversed. Shifts and masks work on values: same result on every machine. Network byte order is big-endian. Convert with htonl/htons (host to network) and ntohl/ntohs (network to host) from <arpa/inet.h>. On a big-endian host they do nothing. It matters when bytes cross machines or tools: network protocols, binary files, memory dumps. Portable code builds bytes with shifts. C++20 std::endian::native (<bit>) tells you the host's order. Endianness is about byte order. Bits inside a byte have no address, so C++ code never sees a \"bit order\". What endianness is Detecting the byte order Network byte order: htonl, htons, ntohl, ntohs Binary files and memory dumps Endian-independent code with shifts Byte order vs bit order Common mistakes little vs big endian stored bytes vs the value htonl vs ntohl byte order vs bit order cout pointer vs raw bytes"},{"k":"question","t":"On a little-endian machine, how is the int 500 (0x000001F4) laid out byte by byte in memory?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q204","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If you cout << somePointer;, do you see the reversed little-endian bytes or the real address?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q205","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is a pointer affected by endianness?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q206","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you detect at runtime whether a machine is little- or big-endian?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q207","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which values are immune to endianness?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q208","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On a little-endian machine (x86-64), what does this print?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q209","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on a little-endian machine, std::cout << x; prints the value of x with its bytes reversed.","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q210","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print (on any machine)?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q211","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is network byte order?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q212","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on a big-endian host, htonl(x) returns x unchanged.","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q213","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A server fills sockaddr_in::sin_port = 8080; without htons on x86-64. Which port does it really use, and why?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q214","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is uint32_t v = *(uint32_t*)buf; a bad way to read a 32-bit number from a received network buffer, and what should you write instead?","c":"19 Endianness","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q215","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/20-undefined-behavior-and-bounds.html","x":"Undefined behaviour means the C++ standard promises nothing: the program may work, crash or quietly compute nonsense. `[]` is unchecked, `.at()` is checked, and a passing run proves nothing. Undefined behaviour (UB): code breaks a rule and the standard sets no requirements on what happens. It may appear to work, crash, corrupt other data, or change with the compiler, flags or input. \"It printed the right value\" is not proof. Common UB: out-of-bounds access, reading an uninitialized variable, null or dangling pointer dereference, use after free, double delete, signed overflow, integer division by zero, data races, falling off the end of a non-void function, shifting by the type's width or more. Undefined (anything goes) vs unspecified (one of several allowed results, undocumented, e.g. argument evaluation order) vs implementation-defined (one documented choice, e.g. sizeof(long)). The optimizer assumes UB never happens and builds on that assumption: it can delete an overflow check or a null check. That is why UB bugs often appear only with -O2. arr[i], std::array::operator[] and std::vector::operator[] do no bounds checking. .at(i) checks and throws std::out_of_range. C++ philosophy: \"don't pay for what you don't use\". [] is fast by default; checking is opt-in with .at() or an explicit if. Find UB with -Wall -Wextra at compile time and sanitizers at run time: -fsanitize=address,undefined -g (lesson 23). What undefined behaviour is Undefined vs unspecified vs implementation-defined Bounds checking: [] vs .at() Why UB is dangerous: the optimizer trusts you Catching UB with sanitizers Common mistakes [] vs .at() undefined vs unspecified unspecified vs implementation-defined UB vs a controlled crash 'works' vs correct signed vs unsigned overflow"},{"k":"question","t":"This program prints 4 and exits cleanly. Is it correct?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q216","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which access is bounds-checked, and what does it do on a bad index?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q217","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does C++ leave operator[] unchecked by default?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q218","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name three different things undefined behaviour is allowed to do.","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q219","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which compiler flag would have caught the out-of-bounds write at runtime during testing?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q220","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"std::array<int, 5> a{}; a[10] = 4; What does the C++ standard say happens?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q221","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q222","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Classify each: (a) signed integer overflow, (b) the order in which f(a(), b()) evaluates a() and b(), (c) sizeof(long). Which is undefined, which unspecified, which implementation-defined?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q223","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"bool ok(int x) { int n = x + 1; return n > x; } returns 0 for INT_MAX at -O0 but 1 at -O2 with g++. Why?","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q224","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are undefined behaviour? Select all that apply.","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q225","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a program with undefined behaviour gives the right output in every test, the undefined behaviour is harmless.","c":"20 Undefined Behavior & Bounds Checking","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q226","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"21 std::array vs std::vector","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/21-array-vs-vector.html","x":"C array, `std::array` or `std::vector`: choose by whether the size is fixed at compile time, and whether it must grow. std::array<T, N> = a C array wrapped in an object: size fixed at compile time, can't grow, no heap allocation of its own. It lives wherever the object lives: stack if local, heap if inside a heap object, static storage if global. std::vector<T> = dynamic array: size chosen and changed at run time (push_back, resize). The elements are always in a separate heap block; the vector object itself is three pointers (24 bytes on x86-64). A C array (int c[N]) decays to a pointer when passed, can't be copied with =, and == compares addresses. std::array fixes all three: it knows its size(), copies with =, and == compares elements. All three are contiguous; std::array and std::vector give .data() for C APIs. Both have [] (unchecked) and .at() (checked, throws std::out_of_range). Pass std::array and std::vector by const&: by value copies every element. std::array<int, 3> and std::array<int, 5> are different types. Fixed, known, small count → std::array. Unknown or changing count → std::vector (the default choice). C arrays mainly for C interop. Large data (megabytes) does not belong in a local std::array: the stack is often 8 MB. Use a std::vector. The three side by side Where std::array lives: it never allocates Passing them to functions How to choose Common mistakes std::array vs std::vector std::array vs C array lives on the stack vs never allocates size vs capacity sizeof(std::array) vs sizeof(std::vector)"},{"k":"question","t":"When should you choose std::array rather than std::vector?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q227","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does a std::vector grow when you keep adding elements?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q228","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a vector's size() and its capacity()?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q229","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Do both array and vector offer a bounds-checked access?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q230","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can grow at runtime with push_back?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q231","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q232","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a std::array<int, 4> that is a member of an object created with new is stored on the heap.","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q233","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On x86-64 with g++, what is sizeof(v) for a std::vector<int> v holding 1000 elements?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q234","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q235","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function takes const std::array<int, 3>&. Why can't you pass it a std::array<int, 5>, and what are two ways to write a function that accepts both?","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q236","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name three things a std::array can do that a C array can't.","c":"21 std::array vs std::vector","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q237","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/22-exceptions-try-catch.html","x":"`throw` reports an error, the nearest matching `catch` up the call chain handles it, and destructors clean up on the way. Uncaught means `std::terminate`. throw raises an exception; control jumps to the nearest matching catch up the call chain, skipping the rest of the try block. .at() throws std::out_of_range on a bad index. An uncaught exception calls std::terminate: \"terminate called after throwing an instance of ...\", then the program aborts. The crash means nobody handled the error. Standard hierarchy: everything derives from std::exception (what() gives the message). logic_error (out_of_range, invalid_argument, length_error) = bugs in the program's logic; runtime_error = conditions only known at run time; bad_alloc = new failed. Write your own exception by deriving from std::runtime_error (or logic_error) and passing the message to its constructor; add fields for extra details. Catch by const& (no copy, no slicing). Order handlers most-derived first; catch (...) catches anything. Inside a handler, throw; rethrows the same object. Stack unwinding: while the exception travels up, every local object in the frames it leaves is destroyed. That is why RAII cleanup works with exceptions (lesson 12). A throwing constructor means the object never existed: its destructor doesn't run, but members already built are destroyed. Destructors must not throw (they are noexcept; a throw calls terminate). noexcept marks functions that never throw. Exceptions for rare errors the direct caller can't handle; error codes / std::optional for expected failures, hot paths and code built without exceptions. throw, try and catch The standard exception hierarchy Your own exception class Several handlers, catch (...) and rethrow Stack unwinding: destructors still run Constructors, destructors and noexcept Exceptions vs error codes Common mistakes uncaught throw vs bug in .at() catch by value vs by reference throw; vs throw e; logic_error vs runtime_error noexcept specifier vs noexcept operator"},{"k":"question","t":"Calling a.at(10) on a size-5 array printed 'Aborted (core dumped)'. Whose fault is the crash?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q238","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Rewrite the call so the program reports the error and keeps running.","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q239","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Should you catch an exception by value or by const reference, and why?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q240","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does e.what() return?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q241","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q242","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which is the recommended way to catch a standard exception?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q243","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q244","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"std::out_of_range, std::invalid_argument and std::length_error all derive from which class?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q245","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a constructor throws, the object's destructor runs to clean up.","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q246","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Inside a catch (const std::exception& e) handler you want to log and pass the error on. Why write throw; and not throw e;?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q247","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must a destructor not throw, and what happens if it does?","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q248","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give one situation where an error code or std::optional is a better choice than an exception, and one where an exception is better.","c":"22 Exceptions & try/catch","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q249","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","c":"C++ Language & Safety","u":"html/23-sanitizers-and-debugging.html","x":"Sanitizers, valgrind and gdb find memory errors, leaks, undefined behaviour and data races while the program runs, and show where they happened. A sanitizer is compiled into the program (-fsanitize=...) and checks memory accesses at run time. It reports a bug only when the bad code actually runs: a clean compile, or a run that never reaches the bug, proves nothing. ASan (-fsanitize=address -g): out-of-bounds (heap, stack, global), use-after-free, double free. It surrounds memory with poisoned redzones tracked in shadow memory, and aborts on the first error. Read an ASan report top-down: the error type, the access (READ/WRITE, size) with its stack, then where the memory came from (which variable, or where it was allocated and freed). Build with -g so it names files and lines. LSan (on with ASan on Linux) reports leaks at exit. UBSan (-fsanitize=undefined) reports signed overflow, bad shifts, null loads... and keeps running. TSan (-fsanitize=thread) reports data races; it can't be combined with ASan. To continue after an ASan error you need both -fsanitize-recover=address at build time and ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=0 at run time. gdb: build with -g -O0, then break, run, next (step over), step (step into), print, bt (backtrace), frame, finish, continue. gdb ./prog core opens a core dump after a crash (ulimit -c unlimited). valgrind (memcheck) runs an unmodified binary and finds invalid reads/writes and leaks (--leak-check=full), but much more slowly. Don't run it on a sanitizer build. Sanitizers cost speed and memory (about 2x slower for ASan on the test below): use them in debug builds and tests, not in production. How sanitizers work and how to build with them ASan: reading a heap-use-after-free report LeakSanitizer: memory that is never freed UBSan: undefined behaviour checks TSan: data races gdb: breakpoints, stepping, backtraces and core dumps valgrind: checking an unmodified binary Which tool finds which bug Common mistakes compile-time vs runtime detection ASan redzone vs stack canary ASan vs valgrind direct vs indirect leak gdb next vs step UBSan vs TSan"},{"k":"question","t":"You built with -fsanitize=address and compiling produced no error — why hasn't AddressSanitizer reported anything yet?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q250","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does AddressSanitizer detect an out-of-bounds access?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q251","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does AddressSanitizer report every error in a run, or stop at the first one?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q252","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name two other tools or flags for finding C++ memory / undefined-behaviour bugs.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q253","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which flags build a program with AddressSanitizer and the line numbers it needs for a readable report?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q254","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a program built with -fsanitize=address compiles without errors, it has no memory bugs.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q255","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"An ASan report says heap-use-after-free ... READ of size 4, then freed by thread T0 here: ... main uaf.cpp:5 and previously allocated by thread T0 here: ... main uaf.cpp:4. What happened, and on which lines?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q256","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which tool reports a data race between two threads incrementing a shared int?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q257","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: running an ASan build with ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=0 is enough to make it report several errors in one run.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q258","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these does valgrind memcheck find in an ordinary build (no sanitizer flags)? Select all that apply.","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q259","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program crashed with a segmentation fault on a test machine, and you can't reproduce it. How do you get a backtrace of that crash with gdb, and what must be in place beforehand?","c":"23 AddressSanitizer & Debugging Tools","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q260","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/24-paging-and-virtual-memory.html","x":"Virtual memory is cut into fixed-size pages and RAM into frames of the same size. A page table per process maps each page to the frame that holds it. A process's virtual address space is cut into equal pages; physical RAM into frames of the same size (4 KiB on x86-64 Linux: getconf PAGESIZE). Each page is stored in one frame. One frame holds exactly one page. The pages of one program (code, heap, stack) can sit in any free frames, in any order. The page table (one per process) maps each virtual page to its frame. Each entry also holds bits: present, writable, user, accessed, dirty, no-execute. The program sees a clean contiguous virtual layout; the pages sit in scattered physical frames. Every pointer you print is a virtual address. Each process has its own page table, so the same virtual address in two processes can hold different data (parent and child after fork). Paging removes the need for one big contiguous block of RAM, so it solves external fragmentation (it leaves a little internal fragmentation, lesson 26). Bonuses: isolation, protection (per-page permissions; a forbidden access ends in a segfault), sharing (one copy of libc's code for all processes) and virtual memory larger than RAM (lesson 27). Pages, frames and the page size The page table: from virtual page to frame What a page table entry holds One page table per process: same address, different data Why paging: benefits and costs Common mistakes page vs frame virtual vs physical address contiguous-virtual vs scattered-physical page table vs /proc/self/maps page fault vs segmentation fault"},{"k":"question","t":"Why is a process divided into pages in the first place?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q261","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a page and a frame?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q262","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Does virtual address space itself store any data or hold any RAM?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q263","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If a program's pages are scattered all over physical RAM, how does it still behave as if memory were one contiguous block?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q264","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Beyond avoiding fragmentation, what benefits does paging unlock?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q265","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: one physical frame can hold pieces of several pages at the same time.","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q266","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which problem does paging solve?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q267","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A global int value = 1; exists. After fork(), the child sets value = 2 and exits. The parent waits for the child, then prints value and &value. What does it see?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q268","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are stored in a page table entry on x86-64? Select all that apply.","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q269","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the page size is 4096 bytes on every Linux system, so it is safe to hardcode it.","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q270","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Writing through a pointer into a string literal, or jumping into code placed on the stack, crashes with a segmentation fault. Which part of paging enforces this, and how?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q271","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"/proc/self/maps shows a region as r-xp. What does each letter mean?","c":"24 Paging & Virtual Memory","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q272","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/25-mmu-tlb-multilevel-paging.html","x":"The MMU translates every virtual address in hardware, the TLB caches recent translations, and a multi-level page table keeps the tables small. The MMU is hardware in the CPU that translates virtual to physical on every access and checks the permission bits. A missing page or a forbidden access raises a page fault for the kernel. A virtual address = page number + offset. With 4 KiB pages the low 12 bits are the offset (kept as-is); the page number goes through the page table to a frame. A flat table would be huge (x86-64: 2^36 entries x 8 B = 512 GiB per process), so multi-level paging uses a tree of small tables (512 entries, 4 KiB each). Only the branches in use exist. x86-64 uses 4 levels: 9+9+9+9 index bits + 12-bit offset = 48 bits (newer CPUs offer 5 levels, 57 bits). The TLB caches recent page-to-frame translations. Hit: no walk. Miss: a page walk of up to 4 memory reads before the real access. A TLB miss is not a page fault. Process switch = new page table, so old TLB entries are wrong: they are flushed, or tagged with an address-space ID (PCID on x86, ASID on ARM). Threads share one page table: no flush. Huge pages (2 MiB or 1 GiB on x86-64): one TLB entry covers 512x more memory, so fewer misses and shorter walks. Linux offers transparent huge pages (THP). Locality: walking memory in order reuses each page, TLB entry and cache line; jumping around (column-wise over a big matrix) misses again and again. The MMU and the address split Multi-level page tables The TLB: caching translations Locality: row-wise vs column-wise traversal Huge pages Common mistakes MMU vs TLB page number vs offset flat vs multi-level page table TLB miss vs page fault 4 KiB page vs huge page"},{"k":"question","t":"What does the MMU do on every memory access?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q273","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A virtual address splits into two parts for translation — what are they, and what does each select?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q274","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why don't OSes use a single flat page table (on x86-64)?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q275","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How many levels does x86-64 paging use, and how is the address split?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q276","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A multi-level walk costs several memory accesses per translation — what hides that cost?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q277","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With 4 KB (4096-byte) pages, virtual address 12290 is in which page, and at what offset?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q278","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With 4 KB pages, how many bits of a virtual address are the offset inside the page?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q279","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (4 KiB pages)","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q280","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: switching between two threads of the same process requires flushing the TLB.","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q281","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do 2 MiB huge pages reduce TLB misses?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q282","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens on a TLB miss, and how is it different from a page fault?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q283","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program's page tables use 52 kB at start. After malloc(1 << 30) (1 GiB) they use 60 kB; after writing to every byte of that block, 2108 kB. Why so little at first, and where do the extra 2 MiB come from?","c":"25 MMU, TLB & Multi-level Paging","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q284","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/26-memory-allocation-and-fragmentation.html","x":"Contiguous allocation, paging and segmentation place memory in different ways; each wastes some of it, inside blocks (internal) or between them (external). Allocation schemes: contiguous (one block per process), and non-contiguous: paging (fixed-size pages, lesson 24) and segmentation (variable-size logical segments). Internal fragmentation = space wasted inside an allocated block, because the block is bigger than the request: the unused end of a region's last page, malloc(13) getting a 24-byte block, a fixed partition bigger than its process. External fragmentation = free memory split into holes between allocated blocks: the total is enough, but no single hole is big enough for the request. Paging cures external fragmentation (any frame fits any page) and keeps internal waste small (under one page per region). Contiguous allocation and segmentation suffer it: external fragmentation is a problem, not a method. Placement strategies for variable-size holes: first-fit (first hole big enough, fast), best-fit (smallest that fits, leaves tiny holes), worst-fit (largest, eats the big holes). Compaction moves blocks together so the holes merge. It needs relocatable addresses (base registers, page tables), so a C/C++ heap, full of raw pointers, can't be compacted. A C++ heap fragments too: headers and rounding (internal), holes between live blocks (external). Reduce it with fewer allocations, pools and arenas. Allocation schemes at a glance Contiguous allocation and placement strategies Internal and external fragmentation Compaction Segmentation Fragmentation inside a C++ heap Common mistakes internal vs external fragmentation paging vs segmentation problem vs method first-fit vs best-fit"},{"k":"question","t":"Where is the wasted space in internal fragmentation versus external fragmentation?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q285","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does paging cause internal fragmentation but eliminate external fragmentation?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q286","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does internal fragmentation only affect the last page of a region?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q287","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is external fragmentation a memory-management method you can choose, like paging?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q288","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does each entry in a segment table store?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q289","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A process needs 10 KB and pages are 4 KB. How many frames does it use, and how much internal fragmentation is there?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q290","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: paging suffers from external fragmentation.","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q291","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Free holes, in address order: 100, 500, 200, 300, 600 KB. A 212 KB request arrives. Which hole does best-fit choose?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q292","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are internal fragmentation? Select all that apply.","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q293","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the allocator can compact a C++ program's heap by moving live blocks together.","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q294","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A segment table has segment 2 with base 4300 and limit 400. What happens for the logical addresses (2, 53) and (2, 450)?","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q295","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A long-running C++ server's memory keeps growing, though it frees what it allocates. Name three ways to reduce heap fragmentation.","c":"26 Allocation Methods & Fragmentation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q296","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","c":"OS Memory & Systems","u":"html/27-demand-paging-and-swapping.html","x":"Pages get a frame only when first touched; when RAM runs short, the kernel evicts cold pages to disk, choosing them with a replacement policy. Demand paging: memory is reserved virtually and gets a frame only on first use. malloc(100 MiB) costs almost nothing until you touch the pages. Touching a page that isn't present raises a page fault: the kernel finds a frame, fills it (zeros, file data or swap), updates the page table and restarts the instruction, invisibly. Minor fault = no disk I/O (fresh zero page, page already in the page cache, copy-on-write). Major fault = the page must be read from disk: far slower. When RAM is full, the kernel evicts a page. Replacement policies: FIFO (oldest), LRU (least recently used), Clock (cheap LRU approximation with a referenced bit), Optimal (farthest future use; the unreachable benchmark). Belady's anomaly: with FIFO, more frames can mean more faults. LRU and Optimal never do this. Copy-on-write: after fork, parent and child share every frame read-only; a page is copied only when one of them writes to it. Swapping: cold anonymous pages go to a swap area on disk so their frames can be reused. Working set bigger than RAM = constant faults = thrashing. Linux overcommits: malloc can succeed for more than is free, because frames come later. If RAM and swap really run out, the OOM killer kills a process. Demand paging and page faults Minor and major page faults Page replacement: FIFO, LRU, Clock, Optimal Copy-on-write after fork Swapping and thrashing Overcommit and the OOM killer Common mistakes demand paging vs swapping minor vs major page fault page fault vs segfault LRU vs Clock Optimal vs LRU swapping vs thrashing"},{"k":"question","t":"Walk through exactly what happens on a page fault.","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q297","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A page fault can be ~1000× slower than a RAM access — why is demand paging still a net win?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q298","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is thrashing, and what causes it?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q299","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between a minor and a major page fault?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q300","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How is a page fault different from a segmentation fault?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q301","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is swapping, and which capability does it provide?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q302","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true of a page fault? Select all that apply.","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q303","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"The running programs need more memory than RAM, so pages are constantly swapped in and out and the system crawls. What is this called?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q304","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With FIFO replacement, the reference string 1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5 gives 9 faults with 3 frames but 10 faults with 4 frames. What is this called?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q305","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"After fork(), the child reads every page of a 40 MiB buffer, then writes every page. Roughly how many page faults does each pass cause in the child?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q306","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on Linux with default settings, malloc(8UL << 30) (8 GiB) can succeed on a machine with 16 GB of RAM even if most of it is in use, and the process's RAM use barely changes.","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q307","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Using LRU with 3 frames, how many page faults does the reference string 1 2 3 1 4 1 5 1 cause?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q308","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do the Clock (second-chance) and Optimal replacement policies choose a victim, and why can't the kernel use Optimal?","c":"27 Demand Paging, Page Faults & Swapping","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q309","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","c":"Linux","u":"html/28-linux-filesystem-navigation.html","x":"Every file sits in one tree that starts at /. Paths are absolute (from /) or relative (from the working directory). One directory tree rooted at /, with no drive letters. Disks and kernel views such as /proc are mounted inside it. An absolute path starts at / and means the same from anywhere. A relative path starts from the current directory. . = here, .. = parent, ~ = your home directory. Every process has a working directory. A relative path in code (std::ifstream(\"config.txt\")) is resolved against it, not against the executable's folder: a classic \"file not found\" bug. Standard directories: /etc configuration, /home users, /tmp temporary, /var logs, /usr/bin programs, /proc processes, /dev devices. Moving around: pwd, cd dir, cd .., cd - (back), ls -la (long format, including hidden names that start with .). File work: mkdir -p, touch, cp -r, mv (move/rename), rm -r (no undo), less, head, tail -f, find (by name), grep -rn (by content). The shell expands wildcards (*, ?) before the command runs. Quote patterns meant for find, and names with spaces. An inode holds a file's data and metadata; a name links to it. Hard link = a second name for the same inode. Symbolic link = a small file holding a path; it breaks when the target goes. One tree: absolute and relative paths The standard directories Essential file commands Hidden files, wildcards and tab completion Inodes, hard links and symbolic links The working directory in a C++ program Common mistakes absolute vs relative path . vs .. working directory vs executable location hard link vs symbolic link"},{"k":"question","t":"Your program does open(\"config.txt\"); it works from one directory but fails with 'file not found' from another. Why?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q310","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between an absolute and a relative path?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q311","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What do . and .. refer to in a path?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q312","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does ls -la show that a plain ls does not?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q313","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Both directories exist. What does this print?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q314","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these paths is absolute?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q315","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"b.txt is a hard link to a.txt (ln a.txt b.txt). You run rm a.txt. What does cat b.txt do?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q316","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true of a symbolic link? Select all that apply.","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q317","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q318","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"The current directory contains a.txt and b.txt. Why does find . -name *.txt fail, and how do you fix it?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q319","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which directory holds system-wide configuration files (the hosts file, the list of users), and which one is a live view generated by the kernel rather than stored on disk?","c":"28 Filesystem & Navigation","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q320","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"29 Permissions (rwx)","c":"Linux","u":"html/29-linux-permissions.html","x":"Every file has an owner, a group and three rwx triplets. The kernel picks one triplet for you and checks it on every access. The ls -l mode: the file type (- file, d directory, l symlink, c/b device), then three rwx triplets for owner, group, others. r=4, w=2, x=1, added per triplet: rwxr-xr-x = 755, rw-r--r-- = 644. stat shows both forms. On a directory: r = list names, w = create, delete, rename entries, x = enter and reach files inside. Deleting a file needs w on the directory, not on the file. Exactly one triplet applies: owner if you own the file, else group if you are a member, else others. No fallback: the owner gets only the owner bits. root skips the read and write checks; to execute, it still needs at least one x bit. A script needs r and x; a binary runs with x alone. umask clears bits from the mode of new files: files start from 666, directories from 777, so umask 022 gives 644 and 755. It is a mask, not a subtraction. Special bits: setuid (runs as the file's owner, e.g. passwd), setgid (runs as the file's group; on a directory, new files get its group), sticky on a directory (only a file's owner may delete it, e.g. /tmp). Reading the mode: ls -l and stat r, w and x on files and directories Which triplet applies, and root umask: the default mode of new files Special bits: setuid, setgid, sticky Common mistakes owner vs group vs others r/w/x on a file vs a directory real vs effective UID umask vs chmod"},{"k":"question","t":"What numeric permission mode does -rwxr-xr-x correspond to?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q321","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You compiled a program but ./prog says 'Permission denied' — what's the most likely cause?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q322","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When you access a file, how does the kernel decide which of the three rwx triplets to apply?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q323","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"On a directory, what do r, w, and x each mean (they differ from a file)?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q324","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is -rw-r--r-- in numeric form?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q325","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"ls -l shows drwxr-x---. What is it, and can others list its contents?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q326","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"The umask is 027. What mode does touch report.txt give the new file?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q327","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a file with mode ----rwxrwx owned by sam can be read by sam, because others are allowed to read it.","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q328","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these need write permission on the directory that holds the file? Select all that apply.","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q329","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What do the setuid bit on an executable and the sticky bit on a directory do? Give one real example of each.","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q330","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A script run.sh has mode --x--x--x. Running ./run.sh fails with \"Permission denied\", but a compiled program with the same mode runs. Why?","c":"29 Permissions (rwx)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q331","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"30 chmod","c":"Linux","u":"html/30-chmod.html","x":"chmod changes a file's permission bits: a numeric mode sets them all at once, a symbolic mode adds, removes or sets only the bits you name. chmod MODE file changes the permission bits (meaning: lesson 29). Only the file's owner or root may do it. Numeric mode sets every bit at once, whatever was there before: chmod 755 f (rwxr-xr-x), 644 (rw-r--r--), 600 (rw-------). Symbolic mode = who (u owner, g group, o others, a all) + operator (+ add, - remove, = set exactly) + bits (rwx). Combine with commas: chmod u+x,g-w,o= f. With no who letter (chmod +x f) the change applies to everyone, except bits set in the umask: under umask 022, chmod +w adds write for the owner only. chmod -R 644 dir strips x from the directories and locks users out. Use chmod -R u=rwX,go=rX dir: capital X adds x only to directories and already-executable files. A fourth leading digit sets the special bits: 4755 setuid, 2775 setgid directory, 1777 sticky directory. chmod -v prints the mode before and after. A missing x is the #1 cause of \"Permission denied\" on ./script.sh. In C/C++: chmod(path, 0644). The leading 0 makes it octal; chmod(path, 644) sets a completely different mode. Numeric modes Symbolic modes: who, operator, bits Numeric or symbolic? Recursive changes: -R and capital X Four-digit modes: the special bits chmod from C++ Common mistakes numeric vs symbolic chmod chmod vs chown x vs X +x vs a+x"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between chmod 755 f and chmod +x f?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q332","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In symbolic chmod, how do you give execute permission to ONLY the owner?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q333","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which permission bit is the usual culprit behind 'Permission denied' when running ./script.sh?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q334","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A file is -rw-r--r-- (644). What does ls -l show after chmod u+x f?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q335","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which command sets a file to exactly rwxr-xr-x, whatever its bits were before?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q336","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A file is -rwxr-xr-x (755). What is its mode after chmod g+w,o-rx f?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q337","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q338","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You want every directory under www/ to be rwxr-xr-x and every regular file rw-r--r--, keeping scripts that are already executable runnable. Which command does it?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q339","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: running chmod 755 dir on a directory whose mode is 2775 leaves the setgid bit set.","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q340","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which numeric mode do you give a shared upload directory where everyone may create files but nobody may delete other people's files, and how does ls -ld show it?","c":"30 chmod","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q341","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"31 chown & Ownership","c":"Linux","u":"html/31-chown-ownership.html","x":"Every file belongs to one user and one group. chown and chgrp change them; only root may give a file to another user. Every file stores an owner (UID) and a group (GID) as numbers; ls -l shows names from /etc/passwd and /etc/group, ls -ln the numbers. Ownership picks the rwx triplet that applies (lesson 29). chown user file, chown user:group file, chown :group file (group only), chown user: file (the user's login group), chgrp group file. -R = recursive. Only root can change a file's owner. The owner may change the group, but only to a group they belong to. A new file gets the creating process's effective UID and primary group, unless the directory has setgid: then it gets the directory's group, and new subdirectories inherit the setgid bit. cp makes a new file owned by you; mv within one filesystem keeps the original owner. Files created with sudo belong to root. id shows your UID, primary GID and all groups (whoami only the name). A new group membership applies from the next login. A service runs as its own system user that can't log in and owns only its data: useradd --system --shell /usr/sbin/nologin webapp, then chown -R webapp:webapp its data directory. chown clears setuid and setgid on an executable, so run chmod u+s after it. A deleted user's files keep the bare UID number. Owner and group are numbers chown and chgrp Who may change ownership Who owns a new file Running a service as its own user Common mistakes chown vs chgrp id vs whoami chown user: f vs chown :group f primary group vs setgid directory"},{"k":"question","t":"Why can't a normal (non-root) user give their own file to another user with chown?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q342","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give the chown syntax for: owner+group, owner only, group only, and recursive.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q343","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do ownership and the rwx bits work together to decide access?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q344","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does id show that whoami does not?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q345","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a regular (non-root) user can give one of their own files to another user with chown.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q346","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which command changes only the group of data.txt to devs?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q347","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"/srv/proj is drwxrwsr-x root devs. User sam (primary group sam, also in devs) runs touch /srv/proj/a.txt. Who owns the new file?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q348","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: chown on a setuid program keeps the setuid bit, as long as root runs it.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q349","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can a regular (non-root) user who owns f.txt do? Select all that apply.","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q350","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A file shows -rw-r--r-- 1 1005 staff ... in ls -l: a number instead of an owner name. What happened, and why can it be a risk?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q351","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why should a server process run as a dedicated system user instead of root, and how is that set up?","c":"31 chown & Ownership","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q352","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"32 Member Initializer List","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/32-member-initializer-list.html","x":"The member initializer list creates each member directly with its value, once, before the constructor body runs; it is also the only way to call a base-class constructor. Written after the constructor's : and before {. It initializes each member directly, once, as the object is built. In the body the members already exist, so a = x; there is only an assignment (default-construct first, then overwrite). Mandatory for const members, reference members, and members whose type has no default constructor. The only way to pass arguments to a base class constructor: Derived(int x) : Base(x) {}. The base is always built first. Members are initialized in declaration order, not the order written in the list (g++ warns with -Wreorder). Never initialize a member from one declared after it. Delegating constructor (C++11): Account(o) : Account(o, 0.0) {} reuses another constructor. It must be the only item in the list, and the target's body runs first. A default member initializer (int seats = 1;) is a fallback, used only by constructors that don't list that member. When the list sets it, the default is not used at all (not even evaluated). Rule of thumb: set every member in the list (or with a default initializer). Keep the body for work that needs the finished members: checks, logging, registration. Initialize, don't assign: the list runs once Members that must use the list Initialization order is declaration order Delegating constructors Default member initializers and the list Common mistakes initialize (list) vs assign (body) declaration order vs list order default member initializer vs list delegating vs normal list"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between initialization (in the list) and assignment (in the body)?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q353","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which members MUST be initialized in the list (won't compile otherwise)?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q354","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you pass an argument to a base class constructor?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q355","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In what order are members initialized?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q356","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a delegating constructor, and its one rule?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q357","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"If C++11 lets you write int a = 0; at the declaration, why is the init list still needed?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q358","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q359","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a member has a default initializer (int a = 0;) and is also in the member initializer list, the list's value is used.","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q360","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q361","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q362","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"class Buffer { int* data; int size; public: Buffer(int n) : size(n), data(new int[size]) {} }; What is wrong?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q363","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When should code go in the constructor body rather than in the member initializer list?","c":"32 Member Initializer List","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q364","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"33 Ternary Operator","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/33-ternary-operator.html","x":"The conditional operator cond ? a : b picks one of two values inside an expression. Real name: conditional operator, the only operator with three operands: cond ? a : b gives a if cond is true, else b. It is an expression (it yields a value), not a statement, so it works inline: initializing a variable or a const, in a return, as a function argument. Only the chosen branch is evaluated, so p ? *p : 0 is safe. Both branches need a common type: int and double give double; int and const char* don't compile. Mixing int and unsigned gives unsigned (-1 becomes 4294967295). Low precedence: inside << wrap it in parentheses, std::cout << (c ? \"a\" : \"b\");. Without them g++ prints c and warns. Use it to choose between two values. For actions, several statements or more than one condition, if/else or switch reads better. Syntax and where it fits The result type: a common type Precedence: parentheses inside << Nesting, and when to use if/else instead Common mistakes ternary vs if/else same type vs common type ?: vs << precedence"},{"k":"question","t":"Is the ternary a statement or an expression, and why does that matter?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q365","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can a ternary initialize a const when if/else can't?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q366","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"In cond ? 1 : 2.5, what is the result type and why?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q367","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When do the two branches make a ternary fail to compile?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q368","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does cout << c ? \"a\" : \"b\"; need parentheses?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q369","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Is the branch that isn't chosen evaluated?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q370","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q371","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"auto r = true ? 1 : 2.5; What is the type of r?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q372","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (unsigned is 32 bits.)","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q373","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q374","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these is the best use of the ternary operator?","c":"33 Ternary Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q375","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/34-creating-objects.html","x":"An object is created in one of three places (the stack, the heap, or static storage), and where you create it decides how long it lives and who destroys it. An object lives in one of three places: the stack (local variables), the heap (new, make_unique) or static storage (globals, static locals, static members: created before main or on first use, destroyed after main returns). The place decides its lifetime (lesson 39). Stack: Entity e; is destroyed automatically at the end of its scope. Fast, but limited in size. Access members with .. Heap: new Entity returns a pointer; the object lives until delete (forget it and you leak). Access members with ->. Today, prefer auto p = std::make_unique<Entity>();, which deletes for you. Use the heap only when needed: the object must outlive its scope, is too big for the stack, its size is known only at run time, or it is used polymorphically. Initialization forms: Entity e(1, 2);, Entity e{1, 2}; (no narrowing), Entity e = {1, 2};, Entity e = Entity(1, 2); (C++17: no copy). Entity e(); declares a function (most vexing parse): write Entity e; or Entity e{};. Entity(1, 2) used as an expression creates a temporary: an object with no name that dies at the end of the full expression. Arrays of objects: Entity a[3]; needs a default constructor; std::vector<Entity> with emplace_back builds elements whose count is known only at run time. Traps: a dangling pointer (to an object already destroyed), a leak (never deleted), a double delete. Set raw pointers to nullptr after delete, or better, don't own objects through raw pointers. Three places an object can live Initialization forms Temporaries: objects without a name Arrays of objects Creating on the heap: new vs make_unique Common mistakes stack vs heap vs static object . vs -> dangling vs leak v(3, 7) vs v{3, 7} temporary vs named object"},{"k":"question","t":"What are the two places an object can live, and how does each affect its lifetime?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q376","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why do you access a heap object with -> but a stack object with .?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q377","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a memory leak and what causes it?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q378","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a dangling pointer? Give two ways to create one.","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q379","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why set a pointer to nullptr after delete?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q380","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does Entity e(); actually declare?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q381","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Entity* e = new Entity; How do you call its print() method?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q382","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these leak memory or use a dangling pointer? Select all that apply.","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q383","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q384","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q385","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these objects live in static storage (alive until the program ends)? Select all that apply.","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q386","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: Entity a[3]; compiles when Entity's only constructor is Entity(int id).","c":"34 Creating / Instantiating Objects","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q387","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"35 The new Keyword","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/35-new-keyword.html","x":"new allocates heap memory and runs the constructor; delete runs the destructor and frees the memory. In modern C++ you rarely write either yourself. new is an operator that does two things: it allocates memory (by calling operator new, which uses malloc in g++'s library) and runs the constructor. It returns a typed pointer. delete is the reverse: destructor, then operator delete (free). Forms: new int (value indeterminate), new int() (zero), new int(5), new int[n] (n may be a run-time value), new int[n]() (all zeros). new Point[3] needs a default constructor, or a brace list for every element. Pairing (a mismatch is undefined behaviour): new/delete, new[]/delete[], malloc/free. Never cross them. new vs malloc: new runs the constructor (and delete the destructor), computes the size itself, returns a typed pointer, and throws std::bad_alloc on failure. malloc gives raw bytes as void* and returns nullptr. Plain new never returns nullptr, so checking for it is useless. new (std::nothrow) T returns nullptr on failure instead of throwing. Heap layout: glibc's allocator keeps a small header just before the block (holding its size), which is how free/delete know how much to release from p alone. For a type with a destructor, new[] also stores the element count (8 extra bytes on x86-64 with g++), so delete[] runs every destructor. (Deep dive: the Heap layout page linked at the bottom of this lesson.) Modern C++: use std::vector instead of new[], and std::make_unique/std::make_shared instead of new (lesson 40). Raw new remains inside RAII classes, placement new, and older or framework code. What new and delete do Forms of new, and arrays new vs malloc When allocation fails: bad_alloc and nothrow Modern C++: when to write new at all Common mistakes new vs malloc delete vs free delete vs delete[] new vs operator new new vs new (std::nothrow) header offset vs block size"},{"k":"question","t":"What two things does new do?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q388","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How does new differ from malloc? (three ways)","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q389","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must new[] be paired with delete[] and not plain delete?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q390","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between delete and free?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q391","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"free/delete are given only p — how do they know where the header is and how big the block is?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q392","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between new int; and new int();?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q393","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q394","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these are true of new compared with malloc? Select all that apply.","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q395","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q396","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program runs out of memory. What does int* p = new (std::nothrow) int[1000]; do?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q397","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: after int* p = new int[1000];, the check if (p == nullptr) is the correct way to detect that the allocation failed.","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q398","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does new Tracked[3] (3 objects of 4 bytes, Tracked has a destructor) ask operator new[] for 20 bytes on x86-64 with g++, while new int[3] asks for 12?","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q399","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Name two places where a raw new is still a reasonable thing to write in modern C++.","c":"35 The new Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q400","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/36-implicit-conversion-explicit.html","x":"The compiler converts values between types on its own: built-in types, one-argument constructors and conversion operators. explicit and brace initialization keep the surprising ones out. A constructor callable with one argument (and not explicit) is a converting constructor: the compiler may use it to turn that argument into your class (Entity e = 22;). Default arguments count: Entity(int a, int b = 0) converts too. Implicit conversion fires in copy-initialization (T x = value;), function arguments and return values. At most one user-defined conversion per chain: Entity e = \"Tom\"; fails when it needs const char* -> std::string -> Entity. explicit turns the implicit use off. Entity e(22);, Entity e{22};, Entity(22) and static_cast<Entity>(22) still work; Entity e = 22; and f(22) don't. Guideline: make one-argument constructors explicit unless the class really is another form of the argument (std::string from const char* is implicit; std::vector's size constructor is explicit). A conversion operator goes the other way: operator double() const turns your object into a double. explicit operator bool() works in conditions (if (c), !c) but not in int n = c;. Built-in conversions can lose data silently: double -> int drops the fraction, a too-big int -> short changes value, -1 -> unsigned becomes 4294967295. -Wall doesn't warn; -Wconversion does. Brace initialization rejects narrowing: int x{3.7}; is an error, while int x = 3.7; quietly gives 3. Converting constructors explicit constructors Conversion operators Built-in conversions and narrowing Common mistakes implicit vs explicit conversion direct init vs copy init one vs two user conversions converting constructor vs conversion operator widening vs narrowing"},{"k":"question","t":"What makes a constructor a 'converting constructor'?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q401","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Exactly why does Entity e = \"Anup\"; fail?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q402","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the explicit keyword do?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q403","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"With an explicit constructor, which works: Entity e = 22; or Entity e(22);? Why?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q404","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is marking single-argument constructors explicit often a good idea?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q405","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Given struct Entity { explicit Entity(int a); }; and void f(Entity);, which lines compile? Select all that apply.","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q406","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q407","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q408","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Class C has explicit operator bool() const, and C c; exists. Which line does not compile?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q409","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (unsigned is 32 bits.)","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q410","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a constructor Entity(int a, int b = 0) can be used for an implicit conversion from int.","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q411","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does int x{3.7}; fail to compile while int x = 3.7; compiles?","c":"36 Implicit Conversion & explicit","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q412","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/37-operator-overloading.html","x":"An operator is a function with a special name. Overloading it gives your own type natural syntax (a + b, a == b, cout << a, v[i]), chosen by the operand types at compile time. An operator is a function with a special name (operator+, operator==, operator<<): a + b calls a.operator+(b) or operator+(a, b). The compiler picks it from the operand types: compile-time polymorphism. Two forms: member (left operand is *this) and free function (both operands are parameters). Use a free function when the left operand isn't your class (std::cout << v, 3 * m). Declare it friend if it needs private members. Return by value for arithmetic (+ makes a new object); return *this by reference for = and compound assignment (+=), so calls chain. operator<< is a free function that takes and returns std::ostream&, so std::cout << a << b chains. Comparisons: ==, < (and C++20's <=>, which can be = default) return bool; std::sort uses <. Mark them const. ++x is T& operator++(); x++ is T operator++(int): a dummy int, and it returns the old copy, so prefix is cheaper. operator[] returns a reference so v[i] = 5 works; provide a const version for const objects. Limits: no new operators, no change of precedence or number of operands, at least one operand of your type; ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof can't be overloaded. Keep the operator's natural meaning. An operator is a function Member, free function, or friend Comparison operators ++ and --: prefix vs postfix operator[] Rules and good style Common mistakes member vs free operator free function vs friend + returns by value vs = returns by reference prefix vs postfix ++ overloadable vs not"},{"k":"question","t":"What is operator overloading, and why is it called compile-time polymorphism?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q413","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When must you use a free (non-member) operator instead of a member?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q414","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must operator<< be a free function and return std::ostream&?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q415","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does operator+ return by value but operator= returns *this by reference?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q416","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two limits on operator overloading.","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q417","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q418","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these operators can not be overloaded?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q419","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q420","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which declaration is the postfix increment (x++) for a class T?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q421","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: an overloaded operator&& still short-circuits, so the right operand is evaluated only when needed.","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q422","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does operator[] usually come in a const and a non-const version?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q423","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a friend function, and why is operator<< often declared as a friend?","c":"37 Operators & Operator Overloading","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q424","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"38 The this Keyword","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/38-this-keyword.html","x":"Inside a non-static member function, this is a pointer to the object the function was called on. this is a pointer available in every non-static member function. It holds the address of the object the function was called on: e.print() passes &e as a hidden argument. Inside a member function, a member name x means this->x. The same code works for every object because this differs per call. Type: Entity* in a normal member function, const Entity* in a const member function (so members can't be changed there). this itself can't be reassigned, which is why it is often described as Entity* const. this = the pointer (the address); *this = the object. this->m is the same as (*this).m. Main uses: tell a member from a same-named parameter (this->name = name;), return *this by reference for chaining, pass the current object to another function (announce(*this)), and the self-assignment check this == &other (lesson 41). Returning *this by value returns a copy: further chained calls change the copy, not the original. this-> is optional for normal member access (it is needed in some template code, covered later). Static member functions have no this. What this is Using this: names, chaining, passing the object on The type of this, and const member functions Common mistakes this vs *this non-const vs const member function this-> optional vs required return *this by reference vs by value"},{"k":"question","t":"What is this, and where is it available?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q425","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the type of this in a non-const vs a const member function?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q426","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between this and *this?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q427","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When do you actually need to write this->?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q428","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static member function have no this?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q429","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q430","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"This version returns Counter by value instead of by reference. What does it print now?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q431","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q432","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q433","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: inside a non-const member function, this = nullptr; compiles.","c":"38 The this Keyword","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q434","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"39 Object Lifetime","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/39-object-lifetime.html","x":"An object's lifetime is the time between its construction and its destruction; its storage duration (automatic, dynamic, static or thread) decides it, and using an object outside its lifetime is undefined behaviour. Lifetime = from the end of construction to the start of destruction (a run-time idea). Scope = where the name can be used (a compile-time idea). They match only for local (automatic) objects. Four storage durations: automatic (locals: until the end of their {} block), dynamic (new: until delete), static (globals, static locals, static members: the whole program), thread (thread_local: one copy per thread, for the thread's life). Automatic objects in one scope are destroyed in reverse order of creation, at a known point: C++ destruction is deterministic, unlike garbage-collected languages. Returning a pointer or reference to a local gives a dangling pointer (its stack frame is gone). Fixes: return by value, return a std::unique_ptr, fill a caller-supplied object, or use an object with static storage. A temporary (an unnamed object such as T(1) or a function's returned value) dies at the end of the full expression, normally the ;. Lifetime extension: binding a temporary directly to a const T& (or T&&) keeps it alive as long as the reference. It does not pass through a function that returns a reference, or through a pointer such as .c_str(). RAII builds on deterministic lifetime: the destructor releases resources at scope exit, even during exceptions (lesson 12). Storage durations: how long an object lives Dangling: using an object after its lifetime Temporaries and lifetime extension Where lifetime extension stops Deterministic destruction Common mistakes scope vs lifetime automatic vs dynamic vs static vs thread dangling vs valid return extended vs not extended temporary RAII vs manual cleanup"},{"k":"question","t":"Name the three storage durations and what determines each object's lifetime.","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q435","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is returning the address of a local variable a bug?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q436","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give two correct ways to return data that outlives a function.","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q437","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is RAII, and why is it exception-safe?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q438","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does a static local variable keep its value between calls?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q439","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (Each destructor prints its id.)","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q440","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which function returns a dangling reference?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q441","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q442","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"const T& pass(const T& t) { return t; } and then const T& bad = pass(T(4));. Is bad safe to use on the next line?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q443","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a thread_local variable has one copy shared by all threads, created when the program starts.","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q444","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is const char* p = (std::string(\"abc\") + \"def\").c_str(); a bug, and how do you fix it?","c":"39 Object Lifetime","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q445","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"40 Smart Pointers","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/40-smart-pointers.html","x":"RAII objects that own a heap object and delete it automatically: unique_ptr for one owner, shared_ptr for shared ownership, weak_ptr to observe without owning. Smart pointers (in <memory>) are RAII wrappers around a raw pointer: they own the heap object and delete it in their destructor, so early returns and exceptions can't leak it. unique_ptr = the only owner. Can't be copied, only moved (std::move). Same size as a raw pointer, practically no run-time cost. Your default; create it with make_unique. shared_ptr = shared ownership through a reference count in a control block; the object is deleted when the last owner goes (use_count()). make_shared needs one allocation instead of two. weak_ptr = a non-owning observer of a shared_ptr; it doesn't change the count. Use .lock() to get a shared_ptr if the object is still alive. It breaks reference cycles. (Deep dive: the shared_ptr circular reference page linked at the bottom of this lesson.) unique_ptr and shared_ptr overload *, -> and the test if (p), so you use them like raw pointers. .get() borrows the raw pointer (never delete it), .reset() deletes now, unique_ptr::release() gives up ownership without deleting. Passing to functions: a function that only uses the object takes T&/const T& (or T* if it may be null); one that takes ownership takes std::unique_ptr<T> by value and the caller writes std::move(p). std::unique_ptr<T[]> owns an array (delete[]), but std::vector is usually better. std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Base>> owns a list of polymorphic objects. Prefer make_unique / make_shared over new: no raw new in your code, and the type is written once. Why smart pointers unique_ptr: one owner Passing smart pointers to functions shared_ptr: shared ownership weak_ptr: observe without owning Arrays and containers Cost and choosing Common mistakes unique_ptr vs shared_ptr weak_ptr vs shared_ptr release() vs reset() vs move owning vs non-owning passing by value vs by reference"},{"k":"question","t":"What problem do smart pointers solve, and which mechanism makes them work?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q446","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between unique_ptr and shared_ptr, and how does shared_ptr know when to delete?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q447","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is weak_ptr for, and why doesn't it affect the reference count?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q448","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why prefer make_unique/make_shared over raw new?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q449","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What's the difference between release(), reset(), and std::move on a unique_ptr?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q450","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When is a raw pointer the right choice over a smart pointer?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q451","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q452","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"auto p = std::make_unique<int>(1); auto q = p; What happens?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q453","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q454","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function only reads an Entity and never stores or deletes it. Callers keep their objects in std::unique_ptr<Entity>. Which parameter type is best?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q455","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q456","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: on x86-64 with g++, sizeof(std::unique_ptr<int>) equals sizeof(int*).","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q457","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must you never create two unique_ptrs (or two independent shared_ptrs) from the same raw pointer?","c":"40 Smart Pointers","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q458","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/41-copy-constructors.html","x":"A class that owns a resource must define how it is copied (deep copy, Rule of Three), may define how it is moved (Rule of Five), and ideally owns nothing raw at all (Rule of Zero). Copy constructor T(const T& other) builds a new object from an existing one: T b = a;, T b(a);, pass by value, return by value (often elided: C++17 builds a returned temporary directly in the caller). The compiler's default copy is shallow (member by member): a pointer member's address is copied, so both objects share one buffer. Shared buffer -> editing one changes the other, and both destructors delete it -> double free (undefined behaviour). A deep copy allocates a new buffer and copies the contents. Copy assignment T& operator=(const T& other) is for two objects that already exist (b = a;): check self-assignment, copy into a new buffer, free the old one, return *this. Rule of Three: a class that needs a custom destructor also needs a custom copy constructor and copy assignment. Rule of Five adds the move constructor and move assignment. Move constructor T(T&& other) noexcept steals the other object's buffer instead of copying it, leaving the source empty. std::move(a) asks for it. Rule of Zero: build classes from members that manage themselves (std::string, std::vector, std::unique_ptr) and write none of the five. Pass by const T& to avoid a copy. = delete forbids copying (like unique_ptr); = default asks for the compiler's version. When copies happen The default copy is shallow Deep copy: a Rule-of-Three String Move constructor (Rule of Five) Rule of Zero, = default and = delete Common mistakes shallow vs deep copy copy constructor vs copy assignment copy vs move Rule of Three vs Five vs Zero = delete vs = default"},{"k":"question","t":"Name two situations where the copy constructor is called.","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q459","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does the default copy constructor do, and why is that dangerous for a class with a pointer member?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q460","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is a deep copy and how does it fix the double-free?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q461","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the Rule of Three (and Five)?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q462","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between the copy constructor and copy assignment?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q463","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (D = default constructor, C = copy constructor, A = copy assignment.)","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q464","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: the compiler-generated copy constructor copies the data that a pointer member points to.","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q465","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print? (D = default constructor, C = copy constructor, M = move constructor.)","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q466","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which special member functions make up the Rule of Five? Select all that apply.","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q467","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which class follows the Rule of Zero safely, with no destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment written by you?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q468","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: if a class's copy constructor is = delete, then T x; f(x); does not compile for void f(T t).","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q469","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does copy assignment in a class that owns a buffer need a self-assignment check (or an allocate-first order)?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q470","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does a move constructor do for a class that owns a heap buffer, and why should it be noexcept?","c":"41 Copying & Copy Constructors","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q471","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"42 The Arrow Operator","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/42-arrow-operator.html","x":"ptr->member is shorthand for (*ptr).member. Smart pointers and iterators overload operator-> so they can be used like pointers. ptr->member is shorthand for (*ptr).member: dereference the pointer, then apply the dot. . is used on an object or a reference; -> on a pointer (raw or smart) or an iterator. . binds tighter than *, so *p.x means *(p.x) and doesn't compile for a pointer p. That is why -> exists. For a raw pointer, -> is built-in. A class can overload operator->: that's how unique_ptr, shared_ptr and iterators support p->member. An overloaded operator-> returns a pointer (or an object that has its own operator->); the compiler keeps applying -> until it reaches a raw pointer. Iterators: it->first on a std::map iterator is (*it).first; it->x on a std::vector<Point> iterator is (*it).x. offsetof(Type, member) (in <cstddef>) gives a member's byte offset. The old &((T*)nullptr)->m trick is undefined behaviour: use the macro. -> is (*p). in one step -> with smart pointers and iterators Overloading operator-> Member offsets with offsetof Common mistakes . vs -> built-in -> vs overloaded -> (*p).x vs *p.x operator-> vs operator*"},{"k":"question","t":"What is e->a equivalent to, written the long way?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q472","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"For a raw pointer, is -> built-in or overloaded — and when does overloading come in?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q473","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does an overloaded operator-> return, and what does the compiler do with it?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q474","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why can you use -> on a smart pointer even though it's an object, not a raw pointer?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q475","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q476","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: an overloaded operator-> must return a pointer, or an object that itself has operator->.","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q477","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q478","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"p is an Entity*. How does the compiler read *p.x?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q479","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q480","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: with Entity& r = obj;, you can write r->x.","c":"42 The Arrow Operator","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q481","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/43-std-vector.html","x":"The default container: a contiguous, growable heap array. Size vs capacity, what a reallocation costs and breaks, insert/erase, and passing vectors cheaply. std::vector<T> = the dynamic array: elements contiguous in one heap block, size changes at run time, freed by the destructor. [] is O(1) and cache-friendly. Which array type to choose: lesson 21. size() = elements stored; capacity() = slots allocated before the next reallocation (spare room = capacity - size). When full, a push allocates a new, bigger block (libstdc++ doubles), moves the elements over and frees the old one. Geometric growth makes push_back amortized O(1): 1,000 pushes move 1,023 elements (growing by 1 slot: 499,500). A reallocation invalidates every pointer, reference and iterator to the elements. Keep an index, or take the pointer again. reserve(n) = room for n, no new elements. resize(n) changes the size. clear() keeps the capacity; shrink_to_fit() asks to free it. push_back(x) copies or moves an existing object in; emplace_back(args...) constructs the element in place (no temporary). [] is unchecked, .at() checked (lesson 20). Iterate with an index, a range-for or iterators. insert/erase in the middle shift every later element: O(n). Erase in a loop with it = v.erase(it); remove all matches with the erase-remove idiom (std::erase_if in C++20). Pass by const& to read, & to modify. Return by value: moved or elided, never copied. A complete program: creating and using a vector Iterating: index, range-for and iterators size, capacity and amortized O(1) growth Reallocation invalidates pointers, references and iterators push_back vs emplace_back insert and erase: cost, and erasing while iterating resize, reserve, clear and shrink_to_fit 2D vectors Passing and returning vectors Common mistakes size() vs capacity() push_back vs emplace_back amortized O(1) vs O(1) reserve() vs resize()"},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between size() and capacity()?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q482","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What happens when push_back exceeds capacity, and why is it still amortized O(1)?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q483","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does reserve() optimize?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q484","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between push_back and emplace_back?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q485","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between v[i] and v.at(i)?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q486","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why are existing iterators/pointers/references invalidated after a vector grows?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q487","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q488","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"You saved int* p = &v[0];, then a push_back made the vector reallocate. What is p now?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q489","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print (g++/libstdc++)?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q490","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q491","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the time cost of v.insert(v.begin(), x) on a vector of n elements?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q492","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these can invalidate a pointer to v[0]? Select all that apply.","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q493","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: returning a local std::vector<int> by value from a function copies all of its elements.","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q494","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"How do you remove every element equal to 0 from a std::vector<int> v in one pass, in C++17 and in C++20?","c":"43 std::vector (Dynamic Array)","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q495","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"44 Local Static Variables","c":"C++ Core","u":"html/44-local-static.html","x":"static on a local variable: the scope stays inside the function, but the lifetime becomes the whole program, so the value survives between calls. static on a local variable changes only its lifetime. Its scope stays inside the function, so it acts like a private global. It lives in the static area (.data when initialized with a non-zero constant, .bss when zero), not on the stack, so it keeps its value between calls. A constant initializer (static int n = 0;) is already in place before main: no run-time work, no flag. A run-time initializer (a constructor, a function call) runs once, the first time execution reaches the line, behind a hidden guard flag. A function that is never called never builds it. The first build is thread-safe since C++11. It is destroyed at program exit, after main returns, in reverse order of construction (the compiler registers the destructor with __cxa_atexit when it is built). Only the first build is thread-safe. Changing the value from two threads afterwards is a data race: use std::atomic or a mutex. There is one per function, not one per object or per call: a static local in a member function is shared by all objects of the class. Uses: counters, values built once on first use (lookup tables, caches), the singleton instance() (lesson 9), and avoiding the cross-file initialization order problem (lesson 8). What static changes on a local variable When it is built, and when it is destroyed Thread safety One per function: uses and drawbacks Common mistakes scope vs lifetime static local vs plain local static int n = 0; vs static Logger L; static Logger L; vs static Logger* P = new Logger(); static local in a member function vs data member"},{"k":"question","t":"What changes when you put static on a local variable, and what stays the same?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q496","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does static Logger L; need a hidden flag but static int n = 0; does not?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q497","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this code print, and why?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q498","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why is returning arr safe with static int arr[3] but not with int arr[3]?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q499","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A function with static Logger L; is never called. Is the constructor ever run? Is the destructor?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q500","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Two threads call a function containing static Logger L; at the same time. What is safe and what is not?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q501","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What is the difference between static Logger L; and static Logger* P = new Logger(); at program exit?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q502","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q503","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: other functions in the same file can use a static local variable by name.","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q504","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q505","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What does this print?","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q506","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which of these does the compiler protect with a hidden guard flag? Select all that apply.","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q507","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Give one advantage and one disadvantage of keeping state in a static local variable.","c":"44 Local Static Variables","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q508","x":""},{"k":"lesson","t":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","c":"Linux","u":"html/45-libraries.html","x":"A library is ready-made compiled code. Static is copied in at link time; shared is found by the loader at run time. A library is ready-made compiled code. Give someone the library and the header and they can call your functions without seeing your .cpp. Static library (.a on Linux, .lib on Windows): an archive of .o files. The linker copies the parts the program needs into it at link time. The program then runs on its own. Shared library (.so on Linux, .dll on Windows): the program only stores the library's name (NEEDED). The loader finds the file and loads it when the program starts, so the .so must be shipped with the program. Both kinds need the header and the library to build. A shared library is needed again at run time. The library goes after the files that use it: g++ main.cpp -L. -lcalc. One shared library serves many programs: its code is loaded once and shared in memory, and fixing it fixes every program. A static library puts its own copy into every program. If the loader can't find it you get cannot open shared object file. Fix: LD_LIBRARY_PATH, a RUNPATH stored in the program (-Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN'), or install the .so in a system folder and run ldconfig. RUNPATH (what modern linkers write) is searched after LD_LIBRARY_PATH and only for the program's direct libraries; the old RPATH is searched before it and for the whole chain. readelf -d shows what the program asks for and where it will look. ldd shows what is found right now. A library hides the source, not the machine code. What a library is Static libraries (.a) Shared libraries (.so) How the loader finds a shared library RPATH vs RUNPATH Inspecting a binary: readelf, ldd, nm, objdump Static vs shared: which to use Common mistakes static vs shared readelf -d vs ldd RPATH vs RUNPATH hidden vs protected -lcalc order and which file wins"},{"k":"question","t":"What is a library, and what does someone need from you to use yours?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q509","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why does g++ -L. -lcalc main.cpp fail with undefined reference, while g++ main.cpp -L. -lcalc works? (static library)","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q510","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program is linked with a static library, then you delete the .a file. What happens when you run the program?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q511","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: a shared library is only needed when the program runs, not when you link it.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q512","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"readelf -d app prints NEEDED  Shared library: [libfoo.so]. What does that tell you?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q513","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Which statements about libraries are true? Select all that apply.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q514","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program has RUNPATH=/opt/v1 and you run it with LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/v2. Both folders hold a libx.so. Which one is loaded?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q515","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program runs on your machine but on the server it prints cannot open shared object file. Give two ways to find out what is wrong and two ways to fix it.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q516","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"What are the two main differences between RPATH and RUNPATH?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q517","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Your CMake project (target_link_libraries(Main Calculator)) built bin/Main, and readelf -d bin/Main shows RUNPATH: .../shared_libraries/lib. You never wrote that. Where did it come from, and what is the catch?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q518","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: ldd is always safe to run on any binary.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q519","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"libcalc.a and libcalc.so are in the same folder and you link with -lcalc. Which one is used?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q520","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"When would you choose a static library, and when a shared one? Give one real reason for each.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q521","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"A program needs libb.so, which needs liba.so. Both are in /opt/libs. The program was linked with -Wl,-rpath,/opt/libs (stored as RUNPATH); libb.so has no path of its own. What does the loader do?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q522","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"Why must code that goes into a shared library be compiled with -fPIC?","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q523","x":""},{"k":"question","t":"True or false: LD_LIBRARY_PATH can make a program load a different copy of a library than the folder stored in its RUNPATH.","c":"45 Libraries: Static and Shared","u":"questions/master-question-sheet.html#q524","x":""},{"k":"deep dive","t":"Heap layout — new / delete / malloc / free","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/heap-layout-new-malloc.html","x":"What sits in memory around a heap block and how delete finds its size."},{"k":"deep dive","t":"shared_ptr circular reference — why it leaks","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/shared-ptr-cycle.html","x":"Why two shared_ptrs pointing at each other never free, and how weak_ptr breaks the cycle."},{"k":"deep dive","t":"From written code to execution","c":"Deep dive","u":"extras/code-to-execution-road.html","x":"End-to-end road from source code through compile, link, load and paging to running instructions."}];